Onboarding is ours generally — and deliberately not ours exclusively

The onboarding split, from this side. `bottles.anecdote.channel` holds the seam; this holds what the
library has to keep straight about its own half.

## The split, and why it is not arbitrary

**The library onboards people. That is the general case and it belongs here.** The exception is a
stranger who encounters a bottle in the wild carrying a control code — they onboard with the bottle,
without having to find us first.

That is not a division of labour that could have gone the other way:

> **A library may be constituency-bounded** — geoJSON, later. A constituency-bounded onboarder cannot
> be the only door, because the person who most needs a door is a stranger holding an artifact, and a
> stranger is in no constituency by definition.

The bottle gets the job by being the **free transit object** — it moves and nothing else does — not
by being suited to governing anything.

## Three things this repository is positioned to get wrong

- **A control code is not ours to issue or revoke.** A bottle exercises authority at minting time and
  never at runtime. If this repo ever wants to revoke one, it has wanted something that does not
  exist.
- **We may be the party that *honours* one.** A code asserts that the holder may be admitted, to
  whoever is later in a position to admit them. A library stacked on a node is a plausible whoever;
  whether it is *the* one is open.
- **Admitting a stranger is not serving a constituency.** If those ever need the same mechanism,
  something has gone wrong — the same shape as no-library-card, and it should be defended by the same
  instinct.

## `OPEN.md` §8 — the larger question the split exposed

**Would a constituency-bounded library still be a library?** Recorded because it is a claim about
*who this library is for*, and nothing here has made one before.

It sits awkwardly against no-library-card (a constituency is not a card and does not gate reading —
but they are close enough that the distinction must be deliberate) and against real-libraries-and-
redundancy (a geographic bound is a different axis and may interact with it).

The question is not whether a library may be bounded. It is **what the bound governs** — whom it
enumerates for, whom it admits, whom it serves bytes to, plausibly three different answers. **Reading
is the one that must not be bounded**, if *no library card* means anything.

Not resolved here. Filed so it is not settled silently as a side effect of the onboarding work.

## Two corrections

- **`BOTTLES.md` is answered.** The repository it specified now exists, so the document is marked as
  the record of what was asked for rather than a live want. It stays because it is the specification
  the stub was written against.
- **The vocabulary table is deleted, not copied.** `bag`/`bottled`/`canonical` live in `bottles` now.
  I removed the table rather than leaving a convenience copy — a copy kept "so a reader is not left
  guessing" is exactly how a second home gets built, which is the failure this constellation keeps
  naming. One consequence is retained in prose because this repository is the one positioned to break
  it: **`canonical` is relational and cannot be computed from a file.**

Also: `consumer` now says **co-mounted, not downstream.** The library and bottles meet on a node —
civic, station, or a pure communications node — and neither is upstream. That is what puts the
onboarding boundary in the right place.

## Not done

- **`.bottles-engine` is still not mounted.** The table records that the repository now exists;
  nothing here depends on it.
- **No third seat.** §7 still stands and this PR does not answer it.
